{
  "dom_id": "step-q47485",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "A baseball is hit by a batter. The distance between the batter and the ball, measured in miles, is given by the function $s(t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $0.015$ | $0.03$ | $0.045$ | $0.05$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity ${s}^{\u2032}(t)$ when $t=1$ using a backward difference approximation.",
  "worked_solution": "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{s}^{\u2032}(t)\u2248\\frac{s(t)-s(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{\u2032}(1) & \u2248\\frac{s(1)-s(1-0.5)}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(1)-s(0.5)}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.03-0.015}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.015}{0.5} \\\\  & =0.03.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is $0.03\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}.$",
